# 550-DRV-0050 — `bcm_wifi` takes `00`, the world domain, under `wlanhw.wit` 1.3 — done

**Type:** driver · **Owner:** `DRV` · **Priority:** P1 · **Status:** done (2026-11-05) · **Blocked by:** — · **Main task:** [550](../issues/550-network-on-real-hardware.md) · **Constitution:** MC-6.1, MC-12.4

## Problem

`wlanhw.wit` 1.3 (550-NET-0035, stage B) says `set-country` takes `00` as well as two capital letters. The station sends `00` when no country is chosen and none is announced. The maintainer said on 2026-11-04 that Wi-Fi must then still work and not go quiet.

`bcm_wifi`'s `Country::from` took only two capital letters, so `00` got `invalid`. With the MacBook Pro and no country known, the station then only listened.

## Plan

1. Take `00` as a code, with the same checks on its limits as any country.
2. Move the country logic out of `serve.rs` into `bcm_wifi/src/country.rs`, with no IDL types in it, so that host tests can run the production code.

## Acceptance criteria

- [x] `00` with the world's limits is applied: probe requests only where it may send, data on every channel it gives.
- [x] A malformed code, a channel twice and a channel not the radio's are still `invalid`.
- [x] `bcm_wifi` builds; the host tests pass.

## Resolution

- `bcm_wifi/src/country.rs` holds `Country` and `CHANNELS` as they were, except that `from` takes `00` and reads `(channel, listen)` pairs. `serve.rs` passes the IDL's limits as pairs and keeps the channel list for `info` (`channel_list`).
- `tests/bcm_country_host.rs` has three cases: the world domain with its listen-only channels, a country's channels, and the refusals. `scripts/host_tests.sh` runs it.
- **Not tested on the MacBook Pro.** On a machine, `00` reaches the radio only when no country is chosen and no access point announces one.

